            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to take a walk, then the Mandarin for to go fishing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: tuōdì",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tuōdì — \"to mop the floor\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ měitiān tuōdì — I mop the floor every day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tuōdì is built from tuō 'to drag' and dì 'ground', both inherited from Middle Chinese, tha and dijH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary glosses the first 'to drag' under an older way of writing it, and notes that it is popularly written the modern way; tha is also the old form of the word for he, a different word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday actions."
            }
          ]
        },
